 in the rings of power story  this is the guy who learns to make the  actual rings of power thanks in part to  the help of a cleverly disguised sauron  eventually calibrimbor learns his  mistake and pays a serious price for  being duped by the dark lord when the  story starts though kelebrimbor is still  a highly respected elven lord with an  impeccable pedigree his family  credentials go straight back to  feyenoord the infamously hot-headed  elven ancestor may have a serious anger  issue but he's also hands down the best  craftsman in all of middle earth history  his grandson caleb brimbore inherits his  skills even if he rejects his  grandfather's darker sins kelebrimbor is  a masterful craftsman and in the source  material he assembles a group of smiths  known as the gwythe mirdine these fellow  craftsmen form a brotherhood that up  until now we haven't heard anything  about toward the end of the trailer we  see a shot of several elven individuals  drawing their swords as if in a ceremony  the timing here is important as the  brief scene comes just after during the  fourth holds up what looks like a piece  of methril the guaithy mirdine set up  shop next to maria to be near the  precious metals so they could import it  for their craft work the timing of the  two moments could mean we're seeing that  group of smiths for the first time the  other possibility here could relate to  feyenoord himself as the elven patriarch  is famous for having his sons swear a  terrible oath perhaps the scene is  depicting that oath in action the man in  the fire has been around since the first  marketing teaser was dropped during the  super bowl the character looks like an  old male human who arrives in a meteor  and he has been shown repeatedly  engulfed in a crater of flames even  though he isn't burning speculation of  who this mysterious figure is has run  rampant since day one and this latest  trailer only adds fuel to the fire one  of the last things we see is the fiery  pit with the man inside along with one  of the harfoots likely nori brandyfoot  he's shown grabbing the ground and then  many of the surrounding rocks begin to  levitate around him then the fire  quickly disappears and the scene cuts  away but just before that happens we see  something the clip is shown from above  for a brief second the figure is in the  center and flames spread outward from  him they make a shape a shape that's  very easy to recognize it looks like a  flaming eye if this isn't a reference to  sauron we don't know what is the idea  that the man is sauron is possible as  the future dark lord does have a bit of  a slow nearly repentant start to the  second age but it could very easily be a  misdirect perhaps this is a proto form  of the eye symbol before it's inherited  by the dark lord it's a mystery that is  only growing as the premiere approaches  the last shot of the trailer follows a  quartet of harfoots as they make their  way down a rolling green slope the  scenery is idyllic iconic middle-earth  stuff some trees can be seen here and  there but by and large the overall feel  is one of hilly rolling grasslands it  feels an awful lot like the veils of  anduin the name of a sprawling area of  middle-earth just east of the northern  misty mountains this is an area that  sees a lot of action over the long years  of middle earth's history many men live  there and elves and dwarves crisscross  it at different points later on bjorn  the skin changer's house is located in  the area as is radagast's home on the  edge of merckwood there's one other  group that lives here for a long while  before they migrate westward too hobbits  earlier groups of hobbit ancestors spend  a long while living in the area the  prologue of the fellowship of the ring  even says their earliest tales seem to  glimpse a time when they dwelt in the  upper veils of anduin between the eaves  of greenwood the great and the misty  mountains could this be the very  beginning of that slow migration into  the veils of anduin there's one thing  that makes this particularly interested  in the theory the mountains away in the  distance of the shot a very long line of  peaks can be seen could this be the  misty mountains to the west with the  eaves of greenwood the great which is  also called mirkwood behind them in the  east it's possible but as with many of  these details only time will shed more  light on the mystery  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  the lord of the rings are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

